response:
 To find the graph of \( y = f^{-1}(x) \), we need to reflect the graph of \( y = f(x) \) over the line \( y = x \). This is because the inverse function essentially swaps the \( x \) and \( y \) coordinates.

Given the graph of \( y = f(x) \):

[asy]
unitsize(0.5 cm);

real func(real x) {
  return (log(x));
}

int i, n;

for (i = -5; i <= 5; ++i) {
  draw((i,-5)--(i,5),gray(0.7));
  draw((-5,i)--(5,i),gray(0.7));
}

draw((-5,0)--(5,0),Arrows(6));
draw((0,-5)--(0,5),Arrows(6));

label("$x$", (5,0), E);
label("$y$", (0,5), N);

draw(graph(func,exp(-5),5),red);

label("$y = f(x)$", (3,-2), UnFill);
[/asy]

We can observe that the graph of \( y = f(x) \) is only in the right half-plane (for \( x > 0 \)) because \( f(x) = \log(x) \) is only defined for \( x > 0 \).

When we reflect this graph over the line \( y = x \), the resulting graph will be:

[asy]
unitsize(0.5 cm);

real func(real x) {
  return (log(x));
}

int i, n;

for (i = -5; i <= 5; ++i) {
  draw((i,-5)--(i,5),gray(0.7));
  draw((-5,i)--(5,i),gray(0.7));
}

draw((-5,0)--(5,0),Arrows(6));
draw((0,-5)--(0,5),Arrows(6));

label("$x$", (5,0), E);
label("$y$", (0,5), N);

draw(graph(func,exp(-5),5),red);
draw((-5,exp(-5))--(5,5),red);

label("$y = f(x)$", (3,-2), UnFill);
label("$y = f^{-1}(x)$", (3,3), UnFill);
[/asy]

Thus, the correct graph of \( y = f^{-1}(x) \) is option D.

\[
\boxed{D}
\]

